Third, if preferred, our bounds on the root-mean-square error \(\epsilon\) can be easily translated to probability bounds. That is, for any \(\delta,\tilde{\epsilon}\), our algorithms succeed in computing the expectation value \(\text{tr}(\mathcal{C}\{\rho\}O)\) to error \(\tilde{\epsilon}\) with high probability \(1-\delta\) over input states \(\rho\) drawn from \(\mathcal{E}\). This follows immediately from Theorems 1 and 2 by an application of Markov's inequality, setting \(\epsilon=\tilde{\epsilon}\sqrt{\delta}\). The run-time of the algorithms remains polynomial and quasi-polynomial, respectively, for any inverse polynomial \(\tilde{\epsilon}\) and \(\delta\). Hence, we say that our algorithms succeed on "most" input states.

### Classical algorithm for quantum circuits with uniform noise

Our algorithm for uniform noise (Algorithm 1) estimates the expectation value, \(\text{tr}(\mathcal{C}\{\rho\}O)=\text{tr}(\rho\mathcal{C}^{\dagger}\{O\})\), by computing an approximation \(\tilde{O}\) of the Heisenberg time-evolved observable \(\mathcal{C}^{\dagger}\{O\}\). This approximation is given by the sum of all low-weight _Pauli paths_ that contribute to \(\mathcal{C}^{\dagger}\{O\}\). Here, a Pauli path \(\vec{P}=(P_{0},\ldots,P_{d})\) is a sequence of Pauli operators corresponding to each layer of the circuit [Fig. 1(b)], and the exact time-evolved operator can immediately be expressed as a sum over Pauli paths,

\[\mathcal{C}^{\dagger}\{O\}=\sum_{\vec{P}}e^{-\gamma\nu[\vec{P}]}\cdot\frac{1} {2^{n}}\text{tr}(OP_{0})\cdot\left(\prod_{t=1}^{d}a_{P_{t-1}P_{t}}^{(t)} \right)\cdot P_{d}, \tag{5}\]

with coefficients given by the transition amplitudes, \(a_{PQ}^{(t)}=(1/2^{n})\text{tr}(QU_{t}^{\dagger}PU_{t})\), at each layer \(t\). Each path is damped by a factor \(e^{-\gamma\nu[\vec{P}]}\) due to noise, where \(w[\vec{P}]=\sum_{t}w[P_{t}]\) is the sum of the individual weights at each circuit layer.

In more detail, we can derive the above formula as follows. Prior to any time evolution, the observable \(O\) can be decomposed in the Pauli basis as \(O=\sum_{P_{0}}(1/2^{n})\text{tr}(OP_{0})P_{0}\). This sets the "initial condition" for the Pauli path. Next, we can time evolve \(O\) through the final circuit layer \(U_{1}\). This produces the time-evolved operator, \(U_{1}OU_{1}^{\dagger}=\sum_{P_{0}}\text{tr}(OP_{0})U_{1}P_{0}U_{1}^{\dagger}\). As before, we can decompose this operator in the Pauli basis to find \(U_{1}OU_{1}^{\dagger}=\sum_{P_{0}}\sum_{P_{1}}(1/2^{n})\text{tr}(OP_{0})\cdot (1/2^{n})\text{tr}(P_{1}U_{1}^{\dagger}P_{0}U_{1})\cdot P_{1}\). Here \(a_{P_{0}P_{1}}^{(t)}=(1/2^{n})\text{tr}(P_{1}U_{1}^{\dagger}P_{0}U_{1})\) is a _transition amplitude_ from the Pauli operator \(P_{0}\) to \(P_{1}\) under the first layer of the circuit. Proceeding in this manner \(d\) times, we obtain Eq. (5), aside from the exponential factor involving \(\gamma\).

To derive the exponential factor, we consider the behavior of the same circuit in the presence of local depolarizing noise. As discussed earlier, the local depolarizing noise channel has a very simple action in the Pauli basis, \(\mathcal{D}\{P\}=e^{-\gamma\nu[P]}P\). Applying this channel at each layer of the circuit above yields an additional overall multiplicative factor of \(e^{-\gamma\nu[P_{0}]}e^{-\gamma\nu[P_{1}]}\cdots e^{-\gamma\nu[P_{d}]}\), which we abbreviate as \(e^{-\gamma\nu[\vec{P}]}\). This completes the derivation of Eq. (5).

Our classical algorithm's approximation for \(\mathcal{C}^{\dagger}\{O\}\) is given by the sum over all Pauli paths with weight below a chosen threshold \(\mathcal{U}\), i.e., all \(\vec{P}\) with \(w[\vec{P}]\leq\mathcal{U}\):

\[\tilde{O}=\sum_{\vec{P}:w[\vec{P}]\leq\mathcal{U}}e^{-\gamma\nu[\vec{P}]} \left(\prod_{t=1}^{d}a_{P_{t-1}P_{t}}^{(t)}\right)P_{d}. \tag{6}\]

From this approximation, we estimate the expectation value of interest as \(\text{tr}(\rho\tilde{O})\).

We hasten to emphasize that our algorithm is in fact identical to those from random circuits [28, 31]. To this end, our ability to rigorously extend the algorithm to general noisy quantum circuits owes to substantial improvements in a number of key proof techniques. In particular, to bound the error in the expectation values, we first prove the following simple lemma.

**Lemma 1**.: Consider a low-average ensemble \(\mathcal{E}=\{\rho\}\) and two observables \(O\) and \(\tilde{O}\). The root-mean-square difference between the expectation value of \(O\) and \(\tilde{O}\) is less than \(||O-\tilde{O}||_{F}\).

Thus, if we are able to bound the Frobenius norm of the sum of truncated Pauli paths, then we can bound the root-mean-square error in our algorithm.

To bound the norm, we group the exponentially many Pauli paths according to their weight at each circuit layer. This organization is naturally viewed via a tree structure [Fig. 2(a)]. Each layer \(t\) of the tree corresponds to a layer of the quantum circuit, ordered from \(t=d,...,1\). At every layer, each vertex branches into up to \(n\) additional vertices, which correspond to Pauli paths with weight \(w_{t}=1,...,n\) at that layer. In this manner, each Pauli path \(\vec{p}\) is uniquely associated with a particular downward sequence of vertices, \((w_{d}),(w_{d},w_{d-1}),...,(w_{d},...,w_{0})\), through the tree.

