* [118] For example, for the entanglement entropy and OTOC, one can consider a deep Clifford circuit. This generates high entanglement despite being classically simulable via the Gottesman-Knill theorem. For the magic, one can consider a 1D circuit with many \(T\) gates but low circuit depth, or a Gaussian fermionic state. Both have an extensive number of \(T\) gates, while remaining simulable via matrix product state and match gate algorithms, respectively. For the operator entanglement entropy (and all aforementioned metrics as well), one can consider a generic highly ergodic many-body system. Time-evolved operators in such systems typically feature a linear growth in operator entanglement entropy over time, despite the operators' expectation values often being efficiently simulated via Pauli truncation methods [46, 49, 50, 51, 54, 55, 56, 57, 58, 59].
